<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yan Fu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1854">1854</a>–<a href="/years/1921">1921</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Ji Dao</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yan Fu was a Chinese military officer, newspaper editor, translator and writer, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fuzhou">Fuzhou</a> in 1854, who introduced Western ideas to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in the late 19th century. A student of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fuzhou-navy-yard">Foochow Arsenal</a> Academy, he went on to the Royal Naval College, Greenwich, in 1877–79 and later taught at the Beiyang Naval Officers' School at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tianjin">Tianjin</a>. After China's defeat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-sino-japanese-war">First Sino-Japanese War</a> he became known for his translations of Thomas Huxley's Evolution and Ethics, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adam-smith">Adam Smith</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-wealth-of-nations">The Wealth of Nations</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-stuart-mill">John Stuart Mill</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/on-liberty">On Liberty</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herbert-spencer">Herbert Spencer</a>'s Study of Sociology, through which natural selection and survival of the fittest reached Chinese readers. He died in 1921.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yan-fu.jpg" alt="Yan Fu" width="330" height="434" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anonymous Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yan_fu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>One of them, <b>Yan Fu</b>, studied at the Royal Naval College at Greenwich and later translated Thomas Huxley and Adam Smith into Chinese.</blockquote><a href="/movement/self-strengthening-movement">Self-Strengthening Movement · People</a></li></ul></section>
